    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Akkadian, the oldest recorded Semitic language, was the language of the empires of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> for more than two and a half thousand years.</li><li>In the 2nd millennium BCE it was the international language of diplomacy, used in the letters exchanged between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>.</li><li>Its literature, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a> and the Babylonian creation epic to the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>, and its records of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a>, medicine and mathematics were recovered when scholars deciphered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> in the 19th century.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1857 the Royal Asiatic Society sent copies of a newly found Assyrian inscription to four scholars, who translated it independently; their versions agreed so closely that the decipherment of Akkadian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> was accepted as proved.</p></aside>
    </section>

