    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2300 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> unites <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and makes Akkadian a language of empire</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1754 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> has his laws inscribed in Akkadian on stone stelae</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">14th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> show Akkadian as the language of diplomacy between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">7th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> gathers his library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">539 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and records his conquest in Akkadian on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 80</span><span class="rf-text">The last datable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablet, an astronomical almanac, is written at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1857</span><span class="rf-text">Four scholars independently translate an Assyrian inscription, proving the decipherment</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2010</span><span class="rf-text">The Chicago Assyrian Dictionary is completed after nearly ninety years of work</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad.jpg" alt="Sargon of Akkad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sargon_of_Akkad_on_his_victory_stele.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ALFGRN, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Akkadian takes its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, or Agade, the city, still unlocated, that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a> made the capital of the first empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> around 2300 BCE. It is the oldest recorded member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a>, the family that includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and Arabic, and the chief representative of their eastern branch, together with Eblaite, the language of the Syrian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a>, known from archives of the 24th century BCE.</p><p>Speakers of Akkadian lived in Mesopotamia alongside the Sumerians from at least the early 3rd millennium BCE, above all in the north. Their names appear in Sumerian texts from about 2600 BCE, and some early kings of the region bore Akkadian names. The two peoples lived so closely together for so long that their languages influenced each other deeply: Akkadian borrowed hundreds of Sumerian words and perhaps its word order, with the verb at the end of the sentence, unusual among Semitic languages.</p><p>Akkadian speakers took over the Sumerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> script and adapted it to their own language, a task made difficult by the differences between the two. Under Sargon and his successors, such as his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a>, royal inscriptions, letters and administrative texts were written in Old Akkadian, and the language spread with the empire across Mesopotamia and beyond.</p><p>After the fall of the Akkadian empire and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>, Akkadian replaced Sumerian as the spoken language of Mesopotamia in the early 2nd millennium BCE, dividing into two main dialects: Babylonian in the south and Assyrian in the north.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="structure">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi.jpg" alt="Code of Hammurabi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P1050763_Louvre_code_Hammurabi_face_rwk.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mbzt, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Like other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a>, Akkadian built its words from roots of usually three consonants, which carried a basic meaning, combined with patterns of vowels, prefixes and suffixes. From the root p-r-s, “to divide” or “decide”, came iprus, “he decided”, iparras, “he decides”, parsum, “decided”, and purussûm, “decision”. Nouns had three cases, marked in the older language by the endings -um, -am and -im, and verbs had a rich system of stems expressing causative, passive and intensive meanings.</p><p>Compared with its Semitic relatives, Akkadian had lost most of the guttural sounds, perhaps under the influence of Sumerian, and placed the verb at the end of the sentence.</p><p>Akkadian was written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, which used several hundred signs in three ways: as syllables, such as ba, ab or bab; as word signs, often the Sumerian word used for its Akkadian equivalent, so that the sign LUGAL, Sumerian for “king”, was read šarrum; and as silent classifiers that marked categories such as gods, cities or men. Many signs had several readings, and reading cuneiform required long training.</p><p>Scholars divide the language into Old Akkadian, of the 3rd millennium BCE, and the Babylonian and Assyrian dialects, each with old, middle and new phases. A literary form known as Standard Babylonian, based on the Old Babylonian of the age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>, was used for literature and scholarship throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in the 1st millennium BCE, much as classical languages were used elsewhere.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="speakers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Speakers &amp; Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kanesh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanesh.jpg" alt="Kanesh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kanesh">Kanesh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%BCltepeOberstadtPalast.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Klaus-Peter Simon, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Akkadian was the language of the great states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> for two thousand years. Assyrian merchants of the early 2nd millennium BCE carried it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, where some 23,000 of their letters and contracts have been found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanesh">Kanesh</a>, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, in the 18th century BCE, ruled an empire whose language of law and government was Old Babylonian, later regarded as the classical form of Akkadian. The palace archives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, of the same period, preserve thousands of letters in Akkadian.</p><p>In the second half of the 2nd millennium BCE, Akkadian became the international language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>. Rulers who spoke Egyptian, Hittite, Hurrian or Canaanite languages corresponded in Akkadian, and scribal schools across the region, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a> in Anatolia to Ugarit on the Syrian coast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, trained scribes to read and write it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, found in Egypt in 1887, contain the correspondence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> with the kings of Babylon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and with the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, almost all in Akkadian.</p><p>In the 1st millennium BCE the Neo-Assyrian and Neo-Babylonian empires, the greatest powers of their time, used Akkadian for their royal annals, inscriptions and administration. Yet their own policies of deporting conquered peoples spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, which gradually replaced Akkadian as the spoken language; Assyrian palace reliefs show scribes writing Aramaic on scrolls beside others writing Akkadian on clay.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> took Babylon in 539 BCE he recorded his conquest in Akkadian, and the Persian kings used it in their inscriptions beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> and Elamite, as in the great trilingual inscription of Darius at Behistun. After the conquests of Alexander, Akkadian survived only among the temple scholars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, who used it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a>, ritual and divination until the 1st century CE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="literature">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Literature &amp; Learning</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh.jpg" alt="Epic of Gilgamesh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Museum_Flood_Tablet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Akkadian has one of the richest literatures of the ancient world. Its masterpiece is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a>, the story of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> who seeks immortality after the death of his friend Enkidu. It was first composed in Old Babylonian, drawing on Sumerian poems, and was later reworked into a standard version of twelve tablets, attributed to the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sin-leqi-unninni">Sin-leqi-unninni</a>, which was found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>’s library; its eleventh tablet tells the story of a great flood.</p><p>Other works include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enuma-elish">Enuma Elish</a>, the Babylonian epic of creation, in which the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> defeats the sea monster Tiamat and creates the world from her body; the Atrahasis epic, with its own account of the creation of humanity and the flood; the Descent of Ishtar to the underworld; and wisdom texts such as the poem of the righteous sufferer, often compared with the Book of Job.</p><p>The laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>, inscribed on a stone stele now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, set out some 280 laws, including the principle of “an eye for an eye”. Thousands of letters, contracts, chronicles and royal annals record the political and daily life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in unprecedented detail.</p><p>Akkadian was also the language of Mesopotamian science. Babylonian scholars compiled vast collections of omens, medical diagnoses and treatments, and mathematical texts, among them the famous tablet Plimpton 322, with its list of numbers related to right-angled triangles. Babylonian astronomers recorded the movements of the moon and planets in astronomical diaries kept for six centuries, from the 7th to the 1st century BCE, and developed mathematical methods to predict them. The library assembled by the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> in the 7th century BCE, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, preserved tens of thousands of tablets of this learning.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="change">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Change &amp; Descendants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription.jpg" alt="Behistun Inscription" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%DA%A9%D8%AA%DB%8C%D8%A8%D9%87_%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%DB%8C%D9%88%D8%B4_(_%D8%A8%DB%8C%D8%B3%D8%AA%D9%88%D9%86(.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Korosh.091, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Akkadian changed over its long history, as its Babylonian and Assyrian dialects developed through their old, middle and new stages. In the 1st millennium BCE it was increasingly displaced as a spoken language by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, which used a simple alphabet of twenty-two letters written with ink on leather, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> and potsherds, and became the common language of the Assyrian, Babylonian and Persian empires. By the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, Akkadian was probably spoken by few if any people.</p><p>It survived as a written language of scholarship and religion in the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, where scribes copied and composed astronomical, astrological and ritual texts. The last datable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablet, an astronomical almanac from Uruk, was written for the year 79 to 80 CE, and then knowledge of Akkadian was lost for some seventeen hundred years.</p><p>Akkadian left no direct descendants, but its influence survived in Aramaic and through it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> and Arabic. The months of the Jewish calendar, among them Nisan, Tammuz and Elul, take their names from the Babylonian months, adopted during the Babylonian exile. Words such as cumin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sesame">sesame</a> probably reached Greek, and through it English, from Akkadian or a related Semitic source.</p><p>The decipherment of Akkadian was one of the great achievements of 19th-century scholarship. Building on the decipherment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a>, scholars used the Babylonian version of the trilingual inscription of Behistun, copied by Henry Rawlinson at great risk on a cliff face in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, to unlock the script. Edward Hincks, Rawlinson, Jules Oppert and William Henry Fox Talbot worked out its principles in the 1850s, and in 1857 their independent translations of the same inscription confirmed their success. In 1872 George Smith of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> discovered the Babylonian flood story on a tablet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, and the excitement it caused made Assyriology famous.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal.jpg" alt="Ashurbanipal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AshurbanipalKingOfAssyria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KeyolTranslater, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Akkadian is the key to three thousand years of Mesopotamian civilisation. Hundreds of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets in Akkadian, from royal archives, temple libraries and private houses, have made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> one of the best-documented societies of the ancient world, with records of its economy, law, religion, science and everyday life.</p><p>Its literature has become part of world literature: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a> is read and translated today as one of the first great works of its kind, and the Babylonian accounts of creation and the flood have transformed the study of the Bible, whose stories they parallel.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">Babylonian astronomy</a>, recorded in Akkadian, passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>: Greek astronomers used Babylonian observations and methods, and the zodiac of twelve signs, with its division into degrees, is a Babylonian creation. The laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> remain one of the most famous legal texts in history.</p><p>The Chicago Assyrian Dictionary, begun in 1921 and completed in 2010, together with other dictionaries and digital corpora, has made Akkadian one of the best-understood languages of antiquity, and new tablets continue to be found and published.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Akkadian, the oldest recorded Semitic language, was the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> from the 3rd millennium BCE to the 1st century CE and used as the diplomatic language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>. Replaced in speech by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, it was recovered by decipherment in the 19th century and reveals the literature, laws and science of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">Babylonian astronomy</a>.</em></p>

